1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,865
Back in Section 0, you watched something satisfying happen.

2
00:00:03,865 --> 00:00:08,333
Five reviewers, one rubric, and a three-point spread collapsed down to one.

3
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:11,550
I want to be honest with you about that demonstration.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:15,959
Those six questions had already been road-tested before you ever saw them.

5
00:00:15,959 --> 00:00:20,308
Draft your own this afternoon, first pass, and there's no such guarantee.

6
00:00:20,308 --> 00:00:25,253
Vague wording produces exactly the disagreement you were trying to remove, just now

7
00:00:25,253 --> 00:00:26,862
wearing a rubric's clothes.

8
00:00:26,862 --> 00:00:31,032
How would you even know, before shipping it, which kind you'd written?

9
00:00:31,032 --> 00:00:33,773
So this lecture isn't about writing questions.

10
00:00:33,773 --> 00:00:36,334
You already have a working method for that.

11
00:00:36,334 --> 00:00:41,041
It's about the part almost nobody does afterwards: proving your rubric actually

12
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:44,913
produces agreement, and fixing it, specifically, when it doesn't.

13
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:47,713
That proof has a name: inter-rater reliability.

14
00:00:47,713 --> 00:00:52,599
By the end of this you'll know how to run it in under an hour, and why the version

15
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:56,829
of this most teams skip is exactly the hour that would have saved them.

16
00:00:57,379 --> 00:01:00,140
Look at the left column, because it's the trap.

17
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,960
Every one of those reads like a rubric question.

18
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:07,894
Each has a subject, each sounds specific, and each will still get a different answer

19
00:01:07,894 --> 00:01:10,186
depending entirely on who's reading it.

20
00:01:10,186 --> 00:01:11,361
Appropriate to whom.

21
00:01:11,361 --> 00:01:12,653
Good by what standard.

22
00:01:12,653 --> 00:01:17,588
The right column is the actual six from Section 0's ticket rubric, close to word for

23
00:01:17,588 --> 00:01:17,881
word.

24
00:01:17,881 --> 00:01:19,761
Notice what they have in common.

25
00:01:19,761 --> 00:01:23,345
Each one points at something you could screenshot and circle.

26
00:01:23,345 --> 00:01:25,930
A date is either in the summary or it isn't.

27
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:29,161
A claim either traces back to the ticket or it doesn't.

28
00:01:29,161 --> 00:01:33,861
That's the whole test for a rubric-grade question: could two people who disagree

29
00:01:33,861 --> 00:01:38,678
about everything else still land on the same answer, because the answer is sitting

30
00:01:38,678 --> 00:01:40,088
right there in the text.

31
00:01:40,088 --> 00:01:43,495
If answering it takes taste, the question isn't ready yet.

32
00:01:44,045 --> 00:01:47,749
Here's the actual protocol, and it's shorter than people expect.

33
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:52,264
Two people, you and one colleague, score the same fifteen cases independently.

34
00:01:52,264 --> 00:01:53,711
Don't discuss them first.

35
00:01:53,711 --> 00:01:58,167
That's the part people skip, and skipping it is what makes the whole exercise

36
00:01:58,167 --> 00:01:58,746
worthless.

37
00:01:58,746 --> 00:02:02,102
Then compare, case by case, not just the final percentage.

38
00:02:02,102 --> 00:02:06,964
A spreadsheet with two columns is the entire tool: your verdicts next to theirs, row

39
00:02:06,964 --> 00:02:07,369
by row.

40
00:02:07,369 --> 00:02:09,048
Wherever you agreed, move on.

41
00:02:09,048 --> 00:02:12,809
Wherever you didn't, stop and read that question again, together.

42
00:02:12,809 --> 00:02:17,555
Nine times out of ten, the wording let two reasonable people read it two different

43
00:02:17,555 --> 00:02:17,845
ways.

44
00:02:17,845 --> 00:02:19,292
Reword that one question.

45
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:20,102
Just that one.

46
00:02:20,102 --> 00:02:22,012
Run the same fifteen cases again.

47
00:02:22,012 --> 00:02:26,005
Clear about eighty-five percent agreement and you're done, freeze it.

48
00:02:26,005 --> 00:02:27,741
Fall short and you loop again.

49
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:30,867
Most rubrics need one or two passes through this loop.

50
00:02:30,867 --> 00:02:32,082
Almost none need ten.

51
00:02:32,632 --> 00:02:37,353
Here's what that loop looks like with real numbers, from exactly this kind of

52
00:02:37,353 --> 00:02:37,782
rubric.

53
00:02:37,782 --> 00:02:41,338
First pass, two reviewers, fifteen cases: nine agreements.

54
00:02:41,338 --> 00:02:42,196
Sixty percent.

55
00:02:42,196 --> 00:02:44,955
Not good enough to hang a launch decision on.

56
00:02:44,955 --> 00:02:50,105
They read through the six disagreements together, and five of the six traced back to

57
00:02:50,105 --> 00:02:52,005
one question: nothing invented.

58
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:55,316
One reviewer was flagging any paraphrase as invention.

59
00:02:55,316 --> 00:02:59,301
The other only flagged claims with no basis in the ticket at all.

60
00:02:59,301 --> 00:03:02,795
Same three words, two different tests hiding behind them.

61
00:03:02,795 --> 00:03:07,945
They reworded it to something narrower: every claim in the summary is traceable to a

62
00:03:07,945 --> 00:03:09,662
specific line in the ticket.

63
00:03:09,662 --> 00:03:11,685
Ran the same fifteen cases again.

64
00:03:11,685 --> 00:03:13,340
Fourteen of fifteen agreed.

65
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:18,490
One bad question was responsible for almost the entire gap, and it took one sentence

66
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:18,919
to fix.

67
00:03:19,469 --> 00:03:22,208
Notice where the definition points the blame.

68
00:03:22,208 --> 00:03:23,425
Not at the reviewer.

69
00:03:23,425 --> 00:03:24,398
At the question.

70
00:03:24,398 --> 00:03:27,563
That's a genuine change of instinct for most people.

71
00:03:27,563 --> 00:03:32,491
Your first reaction to a disagreement is to think the other person read it wrong.

72
00:03:32,491 --> 00:03:37,420
Nearly always, both of you read it correctly, and the question simply allowed two

73
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:38,455
correct readings.

74
00:03:38,455 --> 00:03:43,201
The exercise is that same protocol, in one paragraph you can act on this week.

75
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:46,122
No special software, no data science background.

76
00:03:46,122 --> 00:03:49,651
A colleague, fifteen cases, and forty-five honest minutes.

77
00:03:49,651 --> 00:03:54,580
One caution before you go and do it: don't brief your colleague on what you meant

78
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:55,250
beforehand.

79
00:03:55,250 --> 00:03:57,014
That defeats the entire test.

80
00:03:57,014 --> 00:04:02,065
The rubric has to survive being read cold, because cold is how it will actually get

81
00:04:02,065 --> 00:04:03,282
used once it's real.

82
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:07,813
Say the cost plainly, because raw agreement has a real blind spot.

83
00:04:07,813 --> 00:04:12,879
Picture a feature that's genuinely excellent: ninety-five percent of outputs pass on

84
00:04:12,879 --> 00:04:14,266
any reasonable reading.

85
00:04:14,266 --> 00:04:19,212
Two reviewers could score almost at random and still agree with each other most of

86
00:04:19,212 --> 00:04:22,409
the time, purely because almost everything is a pass.

87
00:04:22,409 --> 00:04:27,234
High agreement, in that case, tells you nothing about whether your rubric is any

88
00:04:27,234 --> 00:04:27,535
good.

89
00:04:27,535 --> 00:04:30,310
You don't need to run that correction by hand.

90
00:04:30,310 --> 00:04:35,256
A number called Cohen's kappa exists specifically to strip out the agreement you'd

91
00:04:35,256 --> 00:04:37,910
expect from chance and show you what's left.

92
00:04:37,910 --> 00:04:42,916
If your pass rate is very high or very low, ask whoever owns your analytics for it.

93
00:04:42,916 --> 00:04:47,982
Anywhere in the middle, roughly thirty to seventy percent, plain agreement is honest

94
00:04:47,982 --> 00:04:49,068
enough on its own.

95
00:04:49,068 --> 00:04:51,058
And budget the discussion itself.

96
00:04:51,058 --> 00:04:53,652
Fifteen cases take twenty minutes to score.

97
00:04:53,652 --> 00:04:58,658
Talking through six disagreements properly, out loud, takes longer than the scoring

98
00:04:58,658 --> 00:04:58,899
did.

99
00:04:58,899 --> 00:05:03,724
That's not overhead, that conversation is where the rubric actually gets better.

100
00:05:04,274 --> 00:05:06,081
One thing before you move on.

101
00:05:06,081 --> 00:05:11,192
The rubric template and the calibration protocol are both in A05, attached to this

102
00:05:11,192 --> 00:05:15,118
lecture, with the fifteen-case loop written out as a checklist.

103
00:05:15,118 --> 00:05:20,167
Run it for real, not on someone else's feature, on the rubric you're building for

104
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:21,475
your own fifty cases.

105
00:05:21,475 --> 00:05:26,524
Find your one bad question this week, while it only costs you an afternoon, not a

106
00:05:26,524 --> 00:05:28,954
launch-week argument nobody can settle.

107
00:05:28,954 --> 00:05:34,002
Next lecture, we hand your rubric to an AI model and ask it to do the scoring for

108
00:05:34,002 --> 00:05:34,252
you.

109
00:05:34,252 --> 00:05:39,238
It works better than you'd expect, and it fails in one specific way that's worth

110
00:05:39,238 --> 00:05:40,983
knowing before you trust it.
